<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mitakshara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mitakshara is a commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a> Smriti, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> law, written by the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijnaneshvara">Vijnaneshvara</a> at the court of the Western Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> in the late 11th or early 12th century. It became the most influential authority on Hindu law in most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, especially on inheritance and joint family property, and British courts applied its rules outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p></div>
